LICENCE NOTES FOR DATA
======================

The termpremia package is released under the MIT licence (see LICENSE).

The datasets bundled with it derive from Federal Reserve Board releases that
assert no copyright and place no restriction on redistribution. Their sources
are recorded below. Data whose terms are more restrictive is downloaded on the
user's behalf and never shipped; see "Notes on data not bundled".


1. gsw_monthly  --  Federal Reserve Board
--------------------------------------------------------------------

Source: Series feds200628, "Nominal Yield Curve"
        https://www.federalreserve.gov/data/nominal-yield-curve.htm

A Federal Reserve Board staff research product. No copyright is asserted on
the source page and no terms of use restrict redistribution. It is not an
official statistical release and is subject to revision without notice.

Derived from the published Svensson parameters rather than copied verbatim:
the curve is re-evaluated on a monthly maturity grid by data-raw/gsw.R.
Please cite the underlying research:

  Gurkaynak, R. S., B. Sack and J. H. Wright (2007). "The U.S. Treasury yield
  curve: 1961 to the present." Journal of Monetary Economics 54(8), 2291-2304.


2. tbill_3m  --  Federal Reserve Board, via FRED
--------------------------------------------------------------------

Source: Federal Reserve Board H.15, distributed as FRED series TB3MS
        https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/TB3MS

A Federal Reserve Board release; no copyright is asserted. Aligned to the
gsw_monthly dates by data-raw/tbill.R.


Notes on data not bundled
--------------------------------------------------------------------

New York Fed ACM term premia (acm_published()) are NOT distributed with this
package. The New York Fed's Terms of Use
(https://www.newyorkfed.org/privacy/termsofuse) do permit copying and
redistribution, but on conditions -- attribution, clearly labelled
modifications, and the same terms passed on to every recipient -- in a licence
that is not a standard open-source licence and that the New York Fed may
change without notice. Shipping the data would have left part of the package
outside the MIT licence its DESCRIPTION declares.

acm_published() therefore downloads the published workbook on the user's
behalf, which puts them in a direct relationship with those terms, and
attaches the required attribution to the result:

    (c) Federal Reserve Bank of New York. Content from the New York Fed
    subject to the Terms of Use at newyorkfed.org.

Nothing that runs on CRAN depends on it. The parser is tested on a synthetic
sheet; the comparisons against the published series run only off CRAN, and
skip when the download fails; and the validation vignette is precomputed by
vignettes/precompile.R, so building it needs neither the data nor a network
connection.

Philadelphia Fed Survey of Professional Forecasters (BILL10, TBILL) is NOT
distributed with this package. The Philadelphia Fed asserts copyright with all
rights reserved and limits use to informational, educational and research
purposes. That is a use restriction rather than a redistribution grant, and it
is incompatible with an MIT-licensed package usable for any purpose. Survey
functionality therefore relies on a download helper plus a clearly-labelled
synthetic fixture for tests.

That is how it is implemented. spf_tbill() and spf_bill10() fetch the data on
the user's behalf, which puts them in a direct relationship with the
Philadelphia Fed's terms; no copy is shipped, no test or vignette depends on
one, and the test suite uses a synthetic survey generated from the model's own
expectations. analysis/validate-spf.R does the real-data validation and is not
part of the built package.
